    <solution><me>\mbox{div}\,{\vec{F}} = 4x + 2y + 6z.</me>
So, if <m>W</m> is the interior of the box, we have
<me>\int_{S}\vec{F} \cdot d\vec{A} = \int_{W}(4x + 2y + 6z)dV
= \int_{3}^{4}\int_{0}^{3}\int_{0}^{2}(4x + 2y + 6z)dz\,dy\,dx.</me>
Integrating through each of the integrals in turn, we have
<me>\int_{S}\vec{F} \cdot d\vec{A} =
\int_{3}^{4}\int_{0}^{3} 4x\,z + 2y\,z + 3z^{2}\bigg|_{z=0}^{z=2}\,
dy\,dx</me>
<me>= \int_{3}^{4}\int_{0}^{3} 8 x + 4 y + 12\,dy\,dx</me>
<me>= \int_{3}^{4} 8 x y + 2 y^2 + 12 y\bigg|_{y=0}^{y=3}\,dx</me>
<me>= \int_{3}^{4} 24 x + 18 + 36\, dx</me>
<me>= 12 x^2 + 18 x + 36 x\bigg|_{x=3}^{x=4}</me>
<me>= 138.</me>




Finding flux directly, note that on the faces <m>x=4</m> and <m>x=3</m> the
normal vectors to the surfaces are <m>\vec i</m> and <m>-\vec i</m> respectively,
so that <m>\vec F\cdot d\vec A = \pm 2x^{2}\,dy\,dz</m> (where <m>x</m> is evaluated
at 4 or 3), and similarly on
<m>y = 3</m> and <m>y = 0</m>, and <m>z = 2</m> and <m>z = 0</m>,
<m>\vec F\cdot d\vec A = \pm y^{2}\,dx\,dz</m> and
<m>\vec F\cdot d\vec A = \pm 3z^{2}\,dx\,dy</m>, respectively.  Thus on
<m>y = 0</m> and <m>z = 0</m> the flux is zero.  On the remaining faces,
we have



(1) On <m>x = 4</m>,
<m>\vec F\cdot d\vec A =
\int_{0}^{3}\int_{0}^{2}\,32\,dz\,dy =
192</m>;



(2) On <m>x = 3</m>,
<m>\vec F\cdot d\vec A =
\int_{0}^{3}\int_{0}^{2}\,18\,dz\,dy =
-108</m>;



(3) On <m>y = 3</m>,
<m>\vec F\cdot d\vec A =
\int_{3}^{4}\int_{0}^{2}\,9\,dz\,dx =
18</m>; and



(4) On <m>z = 2</m>,
<m>\vec F\cdot d\vec A =
\int_{3}^{4}\int_{0}^{3}\,12\,dy\,dx =
36</m>.
Thus the total flux is the sum of these,
<m>\vec F\cdot d\vec A = 138</m>.



</solution>
